{
  "abstract": "We study twenty-vertex graphs with at most 108 edges, no $K_9$, and an independent triple of vertices of degree at least nine, whose deletion leaves a spanning seventeen-vertex core formed from five clique parts with cyclic distance-two joins. Arbitrary additional core edges are allowed. For eleven explicit core types, we prove that nine-colorability is equivalent to avoiding a specified family of three known nineteen-vertex ten-chromatic graphs; six core types have no obstruction, and two classifications give induced containment. Consequently every biplanar extension in these eleven rows is nine-colorable, and no noncolorable extension is ten-vertex-critical. A separate structural theorem covers all twenty-six positive five-part types permitted by the edge and clique bounds: every nineteen-vertex induced subgraph has an independent triple exactly when the graph contains none of the three known sources. A minimal-edge reduction gives 39 attachment orbits and 104 labeled missed-set pairs for the thirteen nonsingleton types; an independent edge-budget argument excludes all thirteen singleton types. Complete coverage checks, 8,210 strict integer inequalities, 520 checked refutations, and exact palette classifiers establish the extension statements. Portable verifiers regenerate the formulas and proof traces. The source classification and its elementary nonbiplanarity criterion are due to Trivedi; the new conclusions concern arbitrary twenty-vertex extensions, which need not be five-cycle blowups. The remaining core types and the unrestricted Earth–Moon problem remain open.",
